    "a": {
      "text": "Right. Three months of data, 135 transactions, and a clear picture already forming around the emergency buffer goal.\n\n---\n\n**The shape of your money is a sawtooth.**\n\nBalance peaks at £1,969, then cycles down to £-503 before the next pay hit. That pattern is consistent — it's not chaos, it's a rhythm. But a rhythm that bottoms out in negative territory means the buffer isn't building between pay cycles. It's being spent down to zero, then some.\n\n---\n\n**The numbers moved in the right direction last month.**\n\nSpend dropped 15% month-over-month — £561 versus £657 the month before. The biggest single shift was in uncategorised spend, which is worth watching: when categories are vague, so is the story. Getting those transactions labelled will sharpen the picture considerably.\n\n[STATS]\nTracked spend | £5,691\nBalance shape | sawtooth\nPeak day | Sun\n[/STATS]\n\n---\n\n**Sundays are doing something specific.**\n\nSunday spend runs at 3.58x the daily average — £2,127 on Sundays versus £594 across other days combined. That's not a rounding error. Something habitual is happening at the weekend that the rest of the week isn't. Worth knowing whether those transactions feel intentional or just... happen.\n\n---\n\n**One structural move would change the sawtooth immediately.**\n\nThe cleanest fix for a pay-cycle that drains to zero is moving money to the buffer *on payday*, before it's visible in the current account. The experiment: transfer a fixed amount toward the emergency buffer on payday for 30 days. Success means the transfer happens and stays put.\n\n[OPTIONS]\n- Yes, let's try it\n- Pick a different one\n- Not right now\n[/OPTIONS]\n\nThere's more in these 135 transactions — this is the surface layer.\n\n— C.",
